KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ read through a pointer to data member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pointer to member as NSDMI: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.mptr.oper]/3-4, [expr.unary.op]/4: `v.*pm' with `int s::*pm =
&s::y' designates v.y.  The front end represents a pointer to data member
as the member's default value cast to a pointer (`pm = (int *)7') and
`v.*pm' as `pm' itself, so the read is wrong.  With a pointer-to-member
default member initializer (`int s::*pm = &s::y;' in u), the declaration
`u w;' disappears from the goto program without a diagnostic, so every
use of w reads an undeclared object.  Found while fixing
cpp11_address_of_member_in_default_member_initializer (round 163h).  No
regression test covered pointers to data members.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept the program.  Pre-existing (fails on bin53).
